# Scoring rubric

This file is hash-locked. Do not change weights, thresholds, or criterion names without a human decision in `state/decisions.md`.

## Thesis tournament (sum = 100)

| Criterion | Weight |
| --- | ---: |
| primary-evidence strength | 25 |
| BIOC1600 biochemical depth | 15 |
| critical insight | 15 |
| reproducibility | 15 |
| central-question relevance | 10 |
| visual explanatory power | 10 |
| Tanner intellectual alignment | 5 |
| novelty without overclaiming | 5 |

Decision rules:

- Discard candidates with total score below 75
- Candidates at or above 85 enter the finalist set
- If none reach 75, keep the highest as `provisional` and say so in the nightly report
- Do not force the slogan thesis

Two independent `poster-red-team` reviewers score every proposal. Record both scores and the mean in `state/scoreboard.json`.

Tanner intellectual alignment means: nucleic-acid recognition honesty, interface/immobilization seriousness, first-year biochemical explainability, and refusal to treat docking as proof. It is not a request to cite Tanner papers for their own sake.

## Analysis tournament

Score each proposed analysis 0–10 on each of the following (no hidden extra criteria):

- scientific relevance
- required assumptions
- availability of public data
- ability to run without paid resources
- reproducibility
- visual value
- ability of first-year students to explain it
- risk of misleading interpretation (higher score = lower risk)

Do not choose molecular docking merely because it produces attractive structures.

InstructNA-scale GPU training is out of scope.

## Meta-improvement A/B

Use this same unchanged rubric. Adopt a prompt patch only if it improves the frozen benchmark and causes no citation, reproducibility, or scientific-integrity regression.
